WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.250
Hello and welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.210
I'm Caro short for Caroline.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.820
I'm a data scientist
at a marketing agency.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.660
I work on a creative tech team.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.990
Basically, that means I
fall down weird rabbit holes

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:19.682
and make cool data toys.

00:00:19.682 --> 00:00:21.890
That is not what I'm here
to talk to you about today,

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:23.120
though.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.030
Here, I am going
to talk about why

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:29.520
we should talk about people who
think they are not data people.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.180
Back in 2020, we all kind
of became armchair experts

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.460
sitting there pondering
in our living rooms

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.160
because we couldn't
go anywhere else.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.250
If you were thinking
of COVID, you and I

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.460
are thinking of the
exact same thing.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.930
All of a sudden there was all
this new jargon that came out.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.980
All these charts, all these
numbers, all these stats,

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.720
case counts.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.898
Flatten the curve
was super popular,

00:00:55.898 --> 00:00:58.190
and if you didn't know what
flattening the curve meant,

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:01.070
you just pretended like you
did so that you could keep up

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:03.110
with everyone else.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:04.080
But everyone was

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.320
all of a sudden--
like our lives were

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.250
up front and personal with data.
It would say whether or not

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.070
we should go out, whether or not
we should see people. Depending

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.630
on the case counts,
would determine,

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:17.580
should we wear a mask?
Should we see our friends?

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.790
Should we stay inside
and do absolutely nothing

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:21.620
else but bake sourdough bread?

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.700
Really,

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.960
data became very, very
up close and personal.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.690
And data have always been
there in the background,

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.770
but it hasn't
always been obvious.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.360
And so in this, I kind
of had this realization

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.520
that not everyone
is interpreting

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.850
data in the same way
that I interpret data.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.680
And there was a lot
of misconceptions

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.240
that this brought up for me.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.210
So that's kind of what
has inspired this.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.130
I want to get started
with this quote,

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.740
because maybe you're sitting
out there thinking, I mean,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.020
I know something
about data, but like,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.100
do I really have
anything to share?

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.400
Yes, you do.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.210
As we become more and
more expert in our field,

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.610
we kind of forget that
not everyone is directly

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.080
behind us following us
down that rabbit hole.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.830
And so it's worthwhile
to look around, remember

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:16.860
our blind spots, and to be able
to share with those around us.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.490
So those misconceptions I
was talking about with COVID,

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:24.510
a lot of people tend to think
data are just charts or just

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:29.220
a bunch of numbers or charts
with stuff and more stuff.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.280
And it's just kind of this
thing that maybe you just think

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.100
about a lot, or maybe
it's really scary

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.890
and data is taking
over, or maybe data

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.520
is just for the nerds.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.510
Like, that's it.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:41.110
It's hard.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.170
We don't need to think about it.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:46.950
And my response to
these things is, Yes

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:51.670
and. Yes data are all of those
things on the previous slide

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.510
and so much more.
In improv comedy,

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.660
there's this theory of
Yes and that you take what

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.020
your partner has given you.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.920
You take the setting, the
characters, maybe the setup,

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.160
and you add to it.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.470
You say Yes and you
add something new.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.560
So I'm going to say
Yes to all of the data

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.130
things on the previous slide.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:11.880
And then also add
something new here.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.890
So we're meeting people
with where they're at

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.190
and where they're coming from.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.700
So yes, to all the things,
and data is creative.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.200
It's not always black and white.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.427
There's a lot of gray.

00:03:21.427 --> 00:03:23.010
Doesn't always have
the right answers,

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.830
which maybe is a terrifying
thing for you to think about.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.290
But it's also super scientific.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.720
It helps us interpret
the world around us

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.060
and understand information
so that we can better

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.300
understand the world that
we live in and ourselves.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.090
And then as you
look around, data

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:42.270
are everywhere, which
makes it really fun,

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.448
at least in my opinion, to
talk about data because they're

00:03:45.448 --> 00:03:46.240
all over the place.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.740
You can ask my
friends and family.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.350
They think they've had way too
many of these conversations.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:54.220
I say never enough.
But it's everywhere.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.460
So, with that in mind,

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.540
you're all formally
invited to a data party.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.270
Yes, I realize we are
already at a data party.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.750
We're at RStudio conf.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.480
So here's yet another
data party invite.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.200
But this one is
just about talking

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.710
about data wherever,
whenever, because it's cool

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.910
and obviously with
a lot of excitement.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.860
If you see, I'm very excited.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:21.310
It's not hard to get me
excited about this stuff.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.230
So let's hop into
this invitation

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.560
of what's really going on
behind this data party.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.050
You might have noticed.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.332
So who are we talking about?

00:04:29.332 --> 00:04:30.290
You might have noticed,

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:34.480
I throw quotes around non datas
a lot. Like, I'm a data person

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.160
or I'm a non data person.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.460
This is intentional.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.370
Usually I think this is
a self designated term

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:45.460
to be a data person
or a non data person.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.990
But really everyone
is a data person.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.710
We just exist somewhere
on this spectrum.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:53.440
So maybe you think
you're closer to novice,

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.670
maybe you think you're
closer to expert.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:58.490
Honestly, any of those
is good and valid.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:01.550
It just matters that we
all recognize we all exist

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.060
somewhere on this spectrum,
and we can meet people

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.350
with where they're at.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.470
I also want to point out that
the line from novice to expert

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:13.160
is probably not straight, so
don't think it's that easy.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.950
There's usually a lot of
rabbit holes, a lot of twists

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.560
and turns along the
way, and there's also

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.820
a lot of different
end paths for expert.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.500
It doesn't mean that we
will necessarily all end

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.600
at the same place,
which is why it's

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.680
good to remember we all have
blind spots. To look around,

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.420
think of what you
see, what you know,

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:34.610
and realize that people
might be on different paths

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.963
and it's worthwhile
sharing things.

00:05:36.963 --> 00:05:38.630
So when you're looking
around for people

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:42.050
to talk to about data,
maybe it could be anyone.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:43.760
If you're working in
an org, maybe it's

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.680
someone who doesn't
work on your team,

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.530
or it's a team that you
work with more obliquely.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.230
So now that we know who
we should be looking for,

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.540
who we should be
talking to, what

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.110
should we talk to them about?

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:58.067
Data but that's a
very broad category.

00:05:58.067 --> 00:06:00.150
So I'm going to give you
some more specific things

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.130
to think about.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.740
So since we're meeting
people where they're at,

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.140
we're saying Yes and we're
taking their data knowledge

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.100
and then adding something to it.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.330
What can we add to it?

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:14.190
I like to think about this
in terms of a grid axis.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.590
It's how I organize my
brain anyways. So we

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.320
can move from less
technical to more technical,

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:21.030
personal to general.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.090
Let's think about
this in terms of music

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.760
to give some practical
examples, because what

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.640
could be educational,
it could be interesting,

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.830
it could be a variety of
things, but this is how

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:33.240
we're going to break it down.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.900
So if we keep it really not too
technical and very personal,

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.780
we can think of Spotify Wrapped
in that lower left quarter

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:40.723
Spotify Wrapped.

00:06:40.723 --> 00:06:41.890
You've probably heard of it.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:43.110
It's the end of year
thing where it's

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.270
like you listened to this
many Spotify artists,

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:46.930
you listened to this
many playlists.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:48.760
This was your top five songs.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:49.750
It's pretty cool.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:50.680
It's pretty fun.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.270
But at the end of the
day, it's just counting.

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:55.520
It's like, how many times
did you listen to this song?

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.450
So pretty simple
data underlying it,

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.370
although it is for
your personal music,

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:03.253
so it's fun to see how yours
is different from your friends.

00:07:03.253 --> 00:07:04.920
If we're looking for
something still not

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.420
too technical, but
more general, Music

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.510
Borders is a really
interesting article put out

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:11.760
by The Pudding, which
if you hadn't haven't

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:14.460
heard of The Pudding, I highly
recommend you check them out.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.670
It's a very interesting
data journalism

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:21.270
outlet that's very fun and
interesting rabbit holes.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.280
This Music Borders
looks at what is

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.350
the most popular
song in your city

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:27.420
or your state or your
country, and then you

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.500
can compare all
around the world.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.260
So what you think
is super popular

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.478
and that hit song people
may have never heard

00:07:33.478 --> 00:07:35.020
about on the other
side of the world.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.520
And they could be listening to
something entirely different,

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.020
which is super fun
to think about as how

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:41.820
music can connect
us and be different,

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:44.212
but also a unifying thing.

00:07:44.212 --> 00:07:45.920
And then if we're
looking more technical,

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.045
if we really want to go
into the weeds with someone

00:07:48.045 --> 00:07:51.470
on some data, maybe we look
at Nadieh Bremer's data art

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.660
that pulls in a lot of
different pieces of data

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:56.750
into a visualization that
really pulls together

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:58.170
some interesting things.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.440
And we can talk about
what goes into a good data

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:02.810
visualization? What
data pieces make

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.750
something really interesting
or really unique,

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:07.370
and look at that?

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:10.790
And then, if we want to go
super technical, The Pudding also

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:13.370
has this really fun thing
that will rant and rave

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:16.290
about your Spotify or your
Apple Music streaming.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.250
It is hilarious, but
under the hood is an AI.

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.200
So you could go deep in
the weeds with someone

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.150
explaining an AI,
what goes into that?

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:27.530
What kind of data are
collected and really

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:30.270
go deeper with someone on that.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.549
So now that we
know some examples

00:08:32.549 --> 00:08:35.490
of what we might be thinking
about as we're presenting,

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.990
where and when should we
be talking to these people?

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:41.580
So if we're talking
something more technical,

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.830
it's probably worthwhile
to maybe set up

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:46.110
an invite with someone,
put it on their calendar

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:49.440
so they know it's coming, and
so they can mentally prepare,

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.910
get excited, maybe send some
documentation in advance.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.620
Really set up for success
for this conversation.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:59.040
Other times, maybe it's
a more casual thing

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.260
and you're hanging out
at a cocktail party

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.150
and you're able
to just small talk

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:07.180
away whatever latest data viz
floated by in your Twitter

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:08.950
that was really
interesting that day.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:12.720
So thinking about where and
when should we have these data

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:15.090
conversations with
folks really depends

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:16.960
on the what.
What are we talking about?

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:18.630
Who are we talking
to and at what

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.105
level do we need to meet them?

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.000
Why are we having this party?

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.810
Let's remember why we're even
going to go through this.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:31.040
So as we had these
misconceptions at the beginning

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.540
or different
conceptions of data.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:38.540
Data are science plus art. It's
the things the two together.

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:41.160
Data are also an
accessibility tool.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.070
I'll give you an example.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:45.450
Let's say, I want to
learn about penguins.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:49.438
I could go to the Antarctic
and stare at penguins all day.

00:09:49.438 --> 00:09:51.230
I would probably learn
a lot about penguins

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:52.710
and see some interesting things.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.970
I would also be very
cold and probably bored,

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:58.070
if I'm being honest.

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:00.260
Slash I have other things I
want to do in my life,

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:03.350
so I will leave the counting
of penguins up to the people

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:06.410
who get super pumped
about penguins.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:08.795
But in that, I can
take their data

00:10:08.795 --> 00:10:10.170
and look at it in
different ways.

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.070
So maybe I will look at their
data and read it in a book

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:17.270
or in some text like this
and see cool longest flipper

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:19.340
length of this penguin is this.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.680
Maybe I would summarize
the data in a table

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.120
and just have the
summary stats like that.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.890
Maybe have a little
bit less context.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:28.820
But I still see the
data in some way.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:32.480
Or maybe I see data as
a chart and all three

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:35.540
of these show the same
kinds of data, but in three

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:38.360
different ways, which is
super important because data

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.630
themselves summarize information
so that we can understand

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:45.300
the world around us and we
can present in different ways.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.570
Data viz and
accessibility in charts

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.730
is a whole other conversation.

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:51.420
I could probably make
this chart better.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.030
I could probably make it
much worse to be accessible.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:57.920
But it's interesting to think
about how we can show data

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.870
and what we can learn
from each of these.

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:01.478
Because you learn
different things,

00:11:01.478 --> 00:11:03.020
or you might realize
different things

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:06.830
by looking at the exact same
data in a different way.

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:09.440
This is also super
important because we're

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.930
not always talking
about penguins,

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:13.470
although we might want to.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:16.400
This also matters because
when we're in our daily lives

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:19.430
and we have news feeds come
up with different stats

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:22.580
or different charts, we want
to be able to understand those

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:25.640
and we want others to be able
to understand them and realize,

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:27.860
what do I need to be
understanding about this chart?

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:29.690
What is the key
piece of information

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:31.550
I need out of this graph?

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:36.000
And then also to realize, is
this chart leading me astray?

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.290
Could this be fake news?

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.070
And being able to realize when
a chart might be misleading

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.780
is a very important
skill to have.

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.890
And so by introducing people
to some simpler concepts

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:48.320
or to even have
these conversations

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:52.400
allows people to realize and be
able to understand data better

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.610
in their daily lives
as they go about them.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:00.770
So maybe you are
totally ready to get out

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:03.860
there, to go for it, to
start talking about data,

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:06.230
and you are pumped.

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:08.970
If that is the case,
go forth, do it.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:12.060
I fully support you. But maybe
you want some more resources.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.130
Maybe you need to sign
up for a newsletter.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:16.730
There's some that will send out
a chart weekly, break it down

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:18.830
for you and make it
really interesting.

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:22.010
You can listen to a
podcast, read a book.

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:24.200
This is one of my
favorite books.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:28.070
You Look Like a Thing and I
Love You by Janelle Shane, it is.

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:29.220
I'm getting some head nods.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.640
It's a hilarious book.

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:35.480
It's all about AI. The title
is a pickup line written

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.550
by AI. So I feel like
that should say enough

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.760
for why you should go
read this or just use

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:42.060
that on your next thing.

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:44.780
You look like a
thing and I love you.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:46.620
Try it out and let me
know how that goes.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.730
I would love to hear
it. But really, this

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:51.230
is just to prepare
you to give you

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:53.630
some thoughts of what to
talk about when you're

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.450
talking with people about data.

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:59.250
And my best advice is really
just go out there and do it.

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:02.030
It might feel super awkward and
super weird at the beginning

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:04.080
and people might give
you some weird looks.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:05.600
Embrace it.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:09.860
Data is super exciting and
we just need to jump in.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:14.000
So for this last thing, I
want to close on a story.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:18.650
So back in 2020, the first
week of working from home,

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:22.080
I was super inspired by
Georgia Lupi's data portraits.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:23.880
I thought, this
is super awesome.

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:26.490
I want to do the exact
same thing for my team.

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:28.940
What a fun thing to
have on our desks when

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:31.550
we go back to the office
like a little name

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:33.330
plate, a little fun

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:34.050
welcome back

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:35.310
thing. Spoiler,

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.240
we never went back
to the office.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:40.290
So I still made them though.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.230
And it was super fun.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:42.985
I made them.

00:13:42.985 --> 00:13:43.860
We talked about them.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:46.220
It was super fun to have
people guess who was who

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:47.850
because I just showed
them this screen.

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.390
And I was like, guess
which one you are?

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:51.348
Some people got it.

00:13:51.348 --> 00:13:52.140
Some people didn't.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:52.650
It was fun.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:53.150
It was fun.

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:54.260
All right.

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:56.660
But it was an interesting
way that we could talk about,

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:59.150
within our data team, to
learn how can we best support

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:01.080
each other while we're
all working remote?

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:02.730
How do we best work remote?

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:05.630
And having those conversations
in a way that maybe you

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:06.900
don't typically have.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:10.040
It wasn't like forced by HR,
but it was this fun, creative,

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:12.050
data driven way.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:15.960
Additionally, our creative lead
caught wind of this project.

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.780
And he's like,
hey, Caro, I heard

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:19.590
you did some cool data thing.

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:21.648
And I was like, Yes.

00:14:21.648 --> 00:14:23.690
And he's like, I'm going
to have you present that

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:26.040
to the entire creative team.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:27.750
And I was like, really?

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:33.170
OK. At the time, I was working
on a team that did primarily

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:35.030
like your classic
marketing analytics,

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:37.680
meaning how many people
clicked on this email?

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.960
How many people should
we target on Twitter?

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:42.270
How should we set up our
tagging specification?

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:45.800
So kind of the bookends of a
project, like we'd help set it up

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:49.550
and then we report on it after.
By presenting this project

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:52.040
to our creative team
that were mostly

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:56.160
what they would self designate
themselves as non data people.

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:57.630
It blew their minds

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.650
that data could be this
creative, this interesting,

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:01.970
this innovative.

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:04.610
And it was really
interesting for me

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:07.430
also because then I got to
have all these conversations

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:11.390
after the fact with people about
how can we incorporate data

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:13.310
in new and innovative
ways that we

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:16.970
hadn't considered incorporating
into our projects previously.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:19.640
And it was really interesting
because we were able

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:21.950
when you're able to let
data out of the box,

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:25.520
to let your creative side and
your data side work together,

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:29.780
we can do so many
cool things with data.

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:33.950
We can do interesting projects
and change the way that we

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:36.570
perceive the world around us.

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:40.010
So getting back to
the point of why is it

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:44.480
beneficial for us data people to
talk to these non data people?

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.760
One, it's good to
help them understand

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:47.700
the world around them

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:49.080
so they can interpret charts.

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:50.490
That's all fine and good.

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:53.360
It is also good for us
because then selfishly

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:55.140
we get to work on cooler stuff.

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:58.160
If people are also
pumped about data and we

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.380
ourselves are
enthusiastic about it,

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:04.370
then we are able to bring these
passions together to share them

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:07.350
and to do really cool things.

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:08.490
So thank you.

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:10.440
Thank you for coming
to my data party.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:13.610
I encourage you to
go start your own.

